According to Rule 18 of the International Regulations for Preventing Collisions at Sea (COLREGs), the vessel constrained by her draught must keep out of the way of the power-driven vessel. This is because Rule 18 specifically states that a vessel constrained by her draught shall keep out of the way of a power-driven vessel. Therefore, the power-driven vessel is not required to take any action to avoid the collision.

Rule 18: Vessel Constrained by Her Draught

- A vessel constrained by her draught shall, so far as possible, keep out of the way of:
  - A sailing vessel;
  - A vessel not under command;
  - A vessel engaged in fishing;
  - A vessel of special construction.

According to Rule 18 (now part of the consolidated text), a power-driven vessel, a vessel not under command, a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre, and a vessel engaged in fishing or fishing with apparatus that restricts manoeuvrability must keep out of the way of a sailing vessel. However, since both the vessel engaged in fishing and the sailing vessel are underway and not specifically mentioned as needing to keep out of the way of each other, we need to refer to the general hierarchy established in Rule 18 (now part of the consolidated text).

From the consolidated text of Rule 18 (which is now part of the general steering and sailing rules):

(a) A power-driven vessel underway shall keep out of the way of:
(i) a vessel not under command;
(ii) a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre;
(iii) a vessel engaged in fishing;
(iv) a sailing vessel.

(b) A sailing vessel underway shall keep out of the way of:
(i) a vessel not under command;
(ii) a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre;
(iii) a vessel engaged in fishing.

(c) A vessel engaged in fishing when underway shall, so far as possible, keep out of the way of:
(i) a vessel not under command;
(ii) a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre.

Given this hierarchy, the vessel engaged in fishing would be required to keep out of the way of the sailing vessel, as the sailing vessel is not specifically listed as needing to keep out of the way of the vessel engaged in fishing. Therefore, the vessel engaged in fishing must keep out of the way according to the Rule 18 hierarchy.

Rule 19(d)(i) prohibits altering to port for a radar contact forward of the beam to avoid a close-quarters situation because doing so could place the vessel in a more dangerous position relative to the other vessel. Specifically, the rule states:

(i) an alteration of course to port, for a vessel forward of the beam, other than for a vessel being overtaken;

This prohibition is aimed at reducing the risk of collision by avoiding maneuvers that might lead to a more hazardous situation. The rationale behind this is that altering course to port for a forward-of-the-beam contact could potentially bring the two vessels closer together or create a more difficult situation to resolve, especially given the limitations and uncertainties associated with radar detection in restricted visibility conditions.

When determining safe speed (all vessels), the following factors shall be among those taken into account:

(a) By all vessels:
(i) the state of visibility;
(ii) the traffic density including concentrations of fishing vessels or any other vessels;
(iii) the manoeuvrability of the vessel with special reference to stopping distance and turning ability in the prevailing conditions;
(iv) at night the presence of background light such as from shore lights or from back scatter of her own lights;
(v) the state of wind, sea and current, and the proximity of navigational hazards;
(vi) the draught in relation to the available depth of water.

These factors are explicitly listed in both Excerpts 1 and 2 from the COLREG reference documents.

The vessel is engaged on pilotage duty and is underway. This conclusion is drawn from the following excerpt:

(a) A vessel engaged on pilotage duty shall exhibit:
(i) at or near the masthead, two all-round lights in a vertical line, the upper being white and the lower red;
(ii) when underway, in addition, sidelight and a sternlight;

The description matches the observed configuration of two all-round white lights in a vertical line (one above the other) at or near the masthead, along with sidelights and a sternlight, indicating that the vessel is engaged on pilotage duty and is underway.

No, according to Rule 18 (a) of the COLREGs, a sailing vessel cannot be the give-way vessel towards a power-driven vessel under normal circumstances. The relevant excerpt states:

(a) A power-driven vessel underway shall keep out of the way of:
(i) a vessel not under command;
(ii) a vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre;
(iii) a vessel engaged in fishing;
(iv) a sailing vessel.

This means that a sailing vessel is always required to keep out of the way of a power-driven vessel, making the power-driven vessel the stand-on vessel and the sailing vessel the give-way vessel.

According to the context provided:

The term 'vessel restricted in her ability to manoeuvre' means a vessel which from the nature of her work is restricted in her ability to manoeuvre as required by these Rules and therefore is unable to keep out of the way of another vessel. The term ‘vessels restricted in their ability to manoeuvre’ shall include but not be limited to:

(i) a vessel engaged in laying, servicing or picking up a navigation mark, submarine cable or pipeline;
(ii) a vessel engaged in dredging, surveying or underwater operations;
(iii) a vessel engaged in replenishment or transferring persons, provisions or cargo while underway.

These are the three examples given in the context.

According to the excerpt from COLREG-Consolidated-2018.pdf, a power-driven vessel underway, when manoeuvring as authorized or required by these Rules, shall indicate that manoeuvre by the following signals on her whistle:

- one short blast to mean "I am altering my course to starboard";

Additionally, this can be supplemented by a light signal of one flash, which has the same meaning.

The most directly relevant Rule 6(b) factor to reducing speed in dense fog, given several targets on radar, is:

(iii) the state of wind, sea and current, and the proximity of navigational hazards;

This factor is part of the broader considerations for determining a safe speed under Rule 6 of COLREG, and in the context of restricted visibility (dense fog), the proximity of navigational hazards becomes particularly critical. However, it's important to note that the primary consideration for reducing speed in this scenario would be Rule 7(a), which states that every vessel shall use all available means appropriate to the prevailing circumstances and conditions to determine if risk of collision exists. If there is any doubt such risk shall be deemed to exist, and then reducing speed to the minimum necessary to maintain steerageway and avoid collision would be a prudent action.

Two Rule 6(a) factors relevant to a small pleasure craft at night without radar are:

(i) the state of visibility;
(iv) at night the presence of background light such as from shore lights or from back scatter of her own lights;

These factors are directly mentioned in Rule 6(a) of the excerpt provided, specifically addressing the considerations a vessel should take into account when determining a safe speed, which includes the state of visibility and the presence of background light at night.
